<p>Among every product we've verified trade data for, groundnut kernels represent the largest total export value — USD 823.2 million across roughly 730,152 MT in calendar 2024 — and the trade concentrates overwhelmingly in Southeast Asia. Indonesia alone imported USD 295.3 million, about 36% of the global total. Add Vietnam (USD 124.9 million), the Philippines (USD 70.3 million), Malaysia (USD 56.2 million) and Thailand (USD 53.9 million), and Southeast Asia accounts for roughly 73% of everything India exports in this category. If you're building a Southeast Asia sourcing relationship with India, groundnut kernels are arguably the clearest anchor product available. This guide covers grades, sourcing season, Indonesian import steps, pricing and the aflatoxin risk that decides most disputes.</p>

<!-- slot:facts -->

<h2>Which groundnut grade suits Indonesian roasters, bold or Java?</h2>
<p>India exports two main groundnut kernel types. Bold (runner-type) kernels are larger and are the more common export grade; Java (Spanish-type) kernels are smaller with a distinct flavour profile suited to different processing applications. Both are HPS (Hand-Picked-Selected) sorted to remove damaged, discoloured or foreign material, then graded by count per ounce, moisture and oil content. Tell us your intended use — snack food processing, confectionery, oil extraction, direct retail — and we'll recommend the appropriate type and grade.</p>

<h2>When is the best time to buy Indian groundnut for Indonesia?</h2>
<p>We source from Gujarat's Saurashtra belt and Andhra Pradesh/Rajasthan, the two dominant Indian groundnut-producing regions. Kharif harvest runs October–December, and HPS processing (sorting, grading, hand-picking) continues through the following months from stored kharif stock — export volume from India is strongest November through March, which is worth factoring into your own ordering calendar if you're timing against Indonesian domestic harvest gaps.</p>
<p>Timing also affects aflatoxin risk. Fresh-crop kernels shipped in the cooler, drier months after harvest have had less time in warm storage than kernels shipped from old stock late in the season, ahead of the next monsoon. If your buyers are sensitive to aflatoxin, weight your annual programme towards the November-March window and test old-crop lots more tightly.</p>

<!-- insight:container-economics -->
<h2>How many tonnes fit in a 20ft container of peanuts?</h2>
<p>About 22-24 MT of bagged kernels in a 20ft and 25-27 MT in a 40ft. Kernels are dense, so both boxes run close to their weight limit, and the 40ft adds only around 3 MT. At the midpoints that is 26 MT against 23 MT, or 1.13 times the cargo. A 40ft is the cheaper option per tonne to Tanjung Priok only if its rate is less than 1.13 times the 20ft rate. Divide each quoted rate by its tonnage and compare.</p>
<p>The value at stake per box is also worth knowing. At Indonesia's 2024 average unit value from WITS (about USD 1.07 per kilogram), a 23 MT 20ft holds roughly USD 24,600 of kernels. One container rejected for aflatoxin therefore costs the whole of that plus re-export or destruction costs, which is why testing a lot before it is loaded is cheap by comparison.</p>
<p>Bag choice is about handling at the roaster. A 23 MT load is 920 bags of 25kg, 460 bags of 50kg or 23 jumbo bags (FIBC). Jute and PP bags let the kernels breathe, which matters on a 12-18 day voyage through humid waters. Our default MOQ is one 20ft container, so a first trial of a new count grade can run on 22-24 MT.</p>
<!-- /insight -->

