<p>Indonesia took about 38% of India's kernels by weight, at a unit value about 5% below the world average, while Thailand and Russia paid about 14-15% more per kilo. Unit values blend every count and contract, so they are not prices. The pattern still tells a buyer something: Indonesian volume leans towards the counts and grades used by roasters, snack makers and processors, bought in full-container programmes, while smaller markets pay more for selected counts and smaller lots. If you sell into premium retail in Indonesia, you are in the smaller, higher-paying segment, and you should specify count and aflatoxin limits accordingly rather than buying from the bulk price.</p>
<!-- /insight -->

<!-- slot:docs -->

<p>The two Indonesian steps are sequential, not interchangeable: food products generally need an ML (Makanan Luar) registration number from BPOM (Badan Pengawas Obat dan Makanan), Indonesia's food and drug regulator, before they can legally be sold, and an SKI (Surat Keterangan Impor) entry permit is then issued for each individual shipment. At the port, Barantin (Badan Karantina Indonesia, the Indonesian Quarantine Agency) carries out quarantine checks on plant products such as groundnut. Indonesia's quarantine authority has suspended imports of Indian groundnut over aflatoxin findings and later lifted that suspension, so check the current status with your importer and Barantin before you contract. Both registration steps are the importer's process, supported by documentation we supply from our side — certificate of analysis, phytosanitary certificate and certificate of origin in particular. Share your compliance checklist and we will confirm the documentation for your shipment.</p>

