<h2>What actually determines the price you're quoted</h2>
<p>We don't quote groundnut kernels off a flat sheet, because five things genuinely move the number and none of them are fixed. Grade is the biggest: Bold and Java are priced differently, and within each, a smaller count number (larger kernel) commands a premium over a larger count number. Aflatoxin-tested lots with a clean certificate of analysis cost more to produce than untested material, because pre-shipment lab testing and the sorting discipline that keeps a lot clean both add cost — but they also protect you from a far more expensive problem at destination. Crop size out of Gujarat and Andhra Pradesh each kharif season swings supply meaningfully, since a weak monsoon reduces both yield and the share of the crop that grades to export quality. Southeast Asian regional demand is also a real driver in its own right — with Indonesia, Vietnam, the Philippines, Malaysia and Thailand together absorbing roughly 73% of India's export volume, a demand shift in any one of those markets has a way of moving pricing for all of them. Currency (INR/USD/IDR) and freight to Jakarta or Surabaya round out the list.</p>
<p>The crop chain runs in a fixed order, and a buyer can follow it. The monsoon sets kharif sowing in Gujarat in June and July. Sowing area tells you the size of the crop months before harvest. From October, new-crop pods arrive at the Saurashtra mandis such as Rajkot and Gondal, where shellers and HPS units buy, and those arrival prices feed straight into kernel offers. The lead indicators are the Gujarat agriculture department's kharif sowing figures in July and August, then arrival volumes and pod prices at Rajkot and Gondal in October and November. A late or patchy monsoon shows in the sowing figures first and in your quote about three months later.</p>
<p>Demand works the same way in reverse. Indonesia takes about 36% of India's kernel exports by value, so when Indonesian buying stops, for example during a quarantine suspension, Indian kernels must find other buyers or go to crushers, and offers to the rest of Southeast Asia soften. When Indonesian buying restarts, the reverse happens. Scenario: a Surabaya roaster who sees strong Gujarat sowing in August and normal Indonesian import flows can expect a well-supplied new crop and book November-December shipments forward with confidence.</p>
<p>Regional competition keeps quotes honest, too. Indonesia, Vietnam and the Philippines all grow groundnut domestically, so Indian supply into this region reflects a genuine cost or volume advantage rather than a captive market — Indian groundnut pricing competes directly against regional alternatives.</p>
<p>Quotes are issued FOB, CIF or CFR against your specific grade, count, aflatoxin-testing requirement, packaging and destination port — never as a flat per-tonne rate. To get an accurate quote fast, tell us: Bold or Java, target count/oz, whether you need aflatoxin testing to a specific limit, packaging preference, quantity and destination port.</p>
